<h2>The loan, the guarantee, the deaths</h2>
<p>Indian Bank sanctioned an educational loan of Rs 4,00,000 in July 2007 to a student and her father, on a promissory note carrying 12.5% interest with quarterly rests, repayable in sixty EMIs after a moratorium. The parents executed a guarantee the same day. The borrower acknowledged the debt in 2010, 2013 and 2016 — keeping limitation alive — but the guarantor father died in 2010 and the mother in 2016. When the bank sued, it impleaded the son, Arun, as the deceased guarantor&rsquo;s legal heir.</p>
<p>Arun applied under Order VII Rule 11 CPC for rejection of the plaint: he had signed no document, received no benefit, held the family property under a settlement deed executed in October 2006 — before the loan — and another heir had not even been impleaded. The trial court dismissed the application, and the revision under Article 227 followed.</p>

<h2>The High Court&rsquo;s reasoning</h2>
<div class="flow">
  <div class="fstep"><strong>Liability follows the estate.</strong> Following the Division Bench authority on deceased sureties, the court held that a guarantor&rsquo;s liability does not fasten on heirs personally; the creditor pursues the estate. The heir &ldquo;may be proceeded against only to the extent of the estate of the deceased, inherited by him.&rdquo;</div>
  <div class="fstep"><strong>No personal liability without a legal basis.</strong> The order is explicit that &ldquo;the second defendant cannot, merely by reason of his status as a legal heir, be made personally liable&rdquo; — personal liability would need an independent foundation, such as his own signature or benefit, which the bank did not allege.</div>
  <div class="fstep"><strong>The plaint survives because it is properly framed.</strong> Since &ldquo;the claim against him is confined to the estate of the deceased guarantor in his hands,&rdquo; the plaint disclosed a cause of action. Order VII Rule 11 looks only at the plaint&rsquo;s averments; the settlement deed and the non-impleadment of the other heir are defences requiring evidence.</div>
  <div class="fstep"><strong>Defences preserved.</strong> The revision was dismissed with all defences kept open, and the court clarified that the dismissal would not amount to a determination of the bank&rsquo;s entitlement to proceed against any specific property.</div>
</div>

<h2>The framework at a glance</h2>
<table class="law">
  <tr><th>Question</th><th>Answer the order gives</th></tr>
  <tr><td>Is the heir of a guarantor personally liable for the debt?</td><td>No — not by reason of heirship alone.</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Can the heir be sued at all?</td><td>Yes, as legal representative — the decree executes against the inherited estate.</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Is a pre-debt settlement deed a threshold defence?</td><td>No — it is a triable issue of fact, to be proved at trial.</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Does non-impleadment of a co-heir defeat the plaint?</td><td>No — that too is an issue for trial, not Order VII Rule 11.</td></tr>
</table>

<h2>Practice notes for both sides</h2>
<div class="check">
  <p>Lenders should plead the representative capacity precisely — a claim framed against the estate in the heirs&rsquo; hands survives threshold attack; a claim framed as personal liability invites one.</p>
  <p>Heirs should answer with an inventory: what devolved, what was independently acquired, and documentary proof of pre-debt transfers. The settlement deed wins cases at trial, not at the Order VII Rule 11 stage.</p>
  <p>Acknowledgments of debt by the borrower keep limitation running against the estate as well — the dates of acknowledgment deserve early scrutiny in every old-loan suit.</p>
  <p>Decree-holders and judgment-debtors alike should ensure the decree itself records the estate limitation, so execution does not stray into the heir&rsquo;s personal assets.</p>
</div>
<div class="note">The order balances two policies that often collide in recovery litigation: banks must be able to follow a debt into a deceased guarantor&rsquo;s estate, and children must not inherit their parents&rsquo; signatures. The line the Madras High Court redraws — representative liability, capped by the estate — is the same line Delhi&rsquo;s courts apply in suits against legal representatives under the CPC, and it decides both how such plaints should be drafted and how they should be defended.</div>
